    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To choose three different marbles from his bag, Johnny can arrange the three marbles.\nThe number of ways to choose three marbles from seven is equivalent to the number that sorts the letters\n\\[\nABC, ACB, BCA, BAC, CAB, CBA\n\\]\nHence, the answer is $\\boxed{6}.$\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nTo make sure this puzzle is solved correctly, we take two steps. First, we figure out how many ways Johnny can make a set from his bag:\n\\[\nAB, BA, AC, CA, BC, CB\n\\]\nThis is just a list of $6$ permutations.\n\nThen, we notice that because alphabetical order is highly significant to us, once we have an ordering that we know to be the same as another ordering, we can order each permutation of them.\nBecause if three different marbles are chosen, it can be arranged in three different ways, an arrangement where one of the three marbles could have been chosen first, another second, and the third third.\nThus, we could describe $AAA, AAB, ABA, BAA,BAB, BAB$.\nSo we have that the number of ways we can choose three different marbles from his bag is $\\boxed{6}$.",
